==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-sit down.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|<,,sat:sate[dated, poetic],sat:sitten[archaic, dialectal]>}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To assume a sitting position from a standing position.
#: {{ux|en|'''Sit down'''! We have work to do.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''sat down''' in the armchair, and my sis '''sat down''' on the floor.}}
#* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|book=Matthew|chapter=9|verse=10|folio=xj|passage=And lo&nbsp;/ many publicans and ſynners&nbsp;/ cam and '''ſatt downe''' alſo with Ieſus&nbsp;/ and his diſciples.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|tlr=Ihon Daus|title=A Famouse Cronicle of Oure Time, Called Sleidanes Commentaries, Concerning the State of Religion and Common Wealth,{{nb...|During the Raigne of the Emperour Charles the Fift, with the Argumentes Set Before Euery Booke, Conteyninge the Summe or Effecte of the Booke Following. Translated out of Latin into Englishe, by Ihon Daus. Here vnto Is Added Also an Apology of the Authoure.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Imprinted at London, by}} Ihon Daye, for Abraham Veale, and Nicholas England|date=25 September 1560|section=folio cccxij, verso|passage=From thence he cometh forth again ⁊ '''ſitteth down''' vnder his cloth of eſtate.}}
#* {{RQ:Plutarch North Lives|chapter=The Life of ''Marcus Antonius''|page=1001|passage=When ſhe knew this galley a farre of, ſhe lift vp a ſigne in the poope of her ſhippe, and ſo ''Antonius'' comming to it, was pluckt vp where ''Cleopatra'' was, howbeit he ſaw her not at his firſt comming, nor ſhe him, but went and '''ſate down''' alone in the prowe of his ſhippe, and ſaid neuer a word, clapping his head betwene both his hands.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=&#91;[[w:William Bradshaw (Puritan)|William Bradshaw]]&#93;|title=A Proposition. Concerning Kneeling in the Very Act of Receiuing Howsoever.{{nb...|Published to Satisfie Professours, yet Humblie, Submitted to the Iudgment of Prophets.}}|year=1605|page=13|passage=Therfore where it is ſuppoſed that Chriſt and his Apoſtles miniſtred & receiued ſitting but by occaſion, and not of purpoſe: becauſe they were ſitting before in eating the paſſeover. wher as if Chriſt had '''ſitten down''' of purpoſ to adminiſter the cõmunion, then all that is ſaid is graunted to be ſome purpoſe.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Galliazzo Gualdo Priorato|tlr=[[w:Henry Carey, 2nd Earl of Monmouth|Henry [Carey, 2nd] Earle of Monmouth]]|title=An History of the Late Warres and Other State Affaires of the Best Part of Christendom, Beginning with the King of Swethlands Entrance into Germany, and Continuing to the Yeare 1640|location=London|publisher=W. Wilson, and are to bee sold by Iohn Hardesty, Thomas Huntington, and Thomas Iackson,{{nb...|at their shops in Ducklane}}|year=1648|pages=340–341|passage={{...}} he made ''Maracini'' advance with his men towards ''Oder'', to hinder ''Orang hells'' further proceedings, or his joyning with ''Bannier'', which if it had happened time enough, that Elector would have had ſomewhat elſe to doe, then to have '''ſitten down''' before a ſtrong Towne which had a prettie Army in the Field to aſſiſt it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=W[illiam] S[mith]|title=An Holy Kiss of Peace, Sent from the Seed of Life, Greeting All the Lambs and Little Ones with a Tender Salutation.{{nb...|With a Few Words; Entituled, Relief Sent Forth Into the Camp of Israel.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} Robert Wilson,{{nb...|at the Sign of the Black-spread-Eagle and Wind-mill, in Martins Le Grand}}|year=1660|page=13|passage={{...}} and then made to thy ſelf a Reſt, and haſt '''ſitten down''' at Eaſe, promiſing to thy ſelf Security in thy profeſſed Formality, where thy Vitals have been ſtopped; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Letters|letter=VIII|date=24 October 1798|page=155|passage=We '''sate down''' to dinner a little after five, and had some beefsteaks and a boiled fowl, but no oyster sauce.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Tales of My Landlord 3|volume=III|chapter=VI|page=274|passage=So saying, she '''sate down''' at a little distance upon the bench on which Allan M‘Aulay was placed, and tuning her clairshach, a small harp, about thirty inches in height, she accompanied it with her voice.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=July and August|author=W. G. Chapman|title=The First of the Exeter Expresses|journal=Railway Magazine|page=215|text="{{...}} my only chance of '''sitting down''' was for the hour we were at dinner. {{...}}."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|_|or|_|reflexive}} To cause to be seated or in a sitting posture; to furnish a seat to.
#: {{ux|en|'''Sit''' your ass '''down''' and shut up.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''sat''' him '''down''' and explained to him how things truly work around here.}}
#* {{RQ:Carlyle Past and Present|2|ch. 6, Monk Samson||Coming home, therefore, I '''sat''' me '''down''' secretly under the Shrine of St. Edmund, fearing lest our Lord Abbot should seize and imprison me.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author=w:A. E. Housman|title=Astronomy|lines=3–4
|passage=Oh I will '''sit''' me '''down''' and weep&nbsp;/ For bones in Africa.}}
#* {{RQ:New Yorker|author=Tad Friend|date=2016-10-03|title=Sam Altman’s Manifest Destiny |url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2016/10/10/sam-altmans-manifest-destiny|passage=Two YC partners '''sat''' Altman '''down''' last year “and told him, ‘Slow down, chill out!’|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261005151321/https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2016/10/10/sam-altmans-manifest-destiny|archivedate=5 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|figurative}} To meet formally at a conference table.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-07-19|author=w:Timothy Garton Ash
|volume=189|issue=6|page=18|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=[http://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2013/jul/10/geopolitics-transatlantic-trade-deal Where Dr Pangloss meets Machiavelli]
|passage=Hidden behind thickets of acronyms and gorse bushes of detail, a new great game is under way across the globe. Some call it geoeconomics, but it's geopolitics too. The current power play consists of an extraordinary range of countries simultaneously '''sitting down''' to negotiate big free trade and investment agreements.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|figurative}} To meet with someone for discussions, negotiations or interrogation.
#: {{ux|en|I '''sat down''' with him about the allegations.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To assume a low or sunken position.
#: {{ux|en|The ball scooted off the fairway and '''sat down''' in the thick rough.}}
#: {{ux|en|As we all climbed aboard, the little boat '''sat down''' low in the water.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|AU}} In Aboriginal use, to [[live]] or [[stay]] at (a place).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1937|year_published=1947|author={{w|Ion L. Idriess}}|title={{w|Over the Range (Idriess book)|Over the Range}}|publisher={{w|Angus and Robertson}}|location=Sydney|page=27|passage=He ‘'''sat down'''’ for days, regaining his strength, quietly sizing up his little kingdom.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1937|year_published=1947|author={{w|Ion L. Idriess}}|title={{w|Over the Range (Idriess book)|Over the Range}}|publisher={{w|Angus and Robertson}}|location=Sydney|page=126|passage=‘He '''sit down''' long time, plenty days, too quiet altogether. All a time he look out young lubra.’}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|AU|obsolete}} To take up [[residence]] in or [[settle]] (an area).

